            </figure>
          </section>

          <section class="cs-section">
            <header class="cs-section-head">
              <div>
                <h3>From camera pixels to eye contact</h3>
                <p class="cs-lead">A camera only sees pixels. To make eye contact, Teleco needs two things:
                  <mark>where you stand</mark> on its map, and <mark>how tall you are</mark>.</p>
              </div>
            </header>
            <ol class="tc-steps">
              <li>
                <b><i class="tc-step-num">01</i>Find people</b>
                <span>My <mark>YOLOv12 pose model</mark> marks where each visitor stands, between their
                  <mark>ankles</mark>.</span>
                <em class="tc-out">→ position in the image</em>
              </li>
              <li>
                <b><i class="tc-step-num">02</i>Place them on the map</b>
                <span><mark>Kabsch alignment</mark>, calibrated by parking Teleco at a few spots, maps camera
                  points onto its <mark>SLAM map</mark>.</span>
                <em class="tc-out">→ x, y · <strong>2–6 cm</strong> error</em>
              </li>
              <li>
                <b><i class="tc-step-num">03</i>Look them in the eye</b>
                <span>A curve fitted on Teleco's known height turns <mark>pixel height</mark> into real
                  height.</span>
                <em class="tc-out">→ height · <strong>&lt;10 cm</strong> error</em>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </section>

          <section class="cs-section">
            <header class="cs-section-head">
              <div>
                <h3>Why a camera in the room, not on the robot</h3>
                <p class="cs-lead">A camera on Teleco only sees what is in front of it. One fixed camera
                  overlooking the area sees <mark>everyone around the robot</mark>.</p>
              </div>
            </header>
            <div class="cs-parts cs-parts-2">
              <div>
                <h5>No blind spots</h5>
                <p>Visitors are tracked on <mark>either side of the robot</mark>, even when Teleco partly hides
                  them from view.</p>
              </div>
              <div>
